1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,104
Two people walked into the same party.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,115
One left feeling like the night gave him

3
00:00:07,115 --> 00:00:07,504
life.

4
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:09,416
Four hours later…

5
00:00:09,416 --> 00:00:12,248
the other couldn't wait to escape.

6
00:00:12,398 --> 00:00:14,702
Neither hated the people there.

7
00:00:14,852 --> 00:00:16,460
Neither had a terrible night.

8
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,264
So why did the exact same experience feel

9
00:00:20,264 --> 00:00:21,080
so different?

10
00:00:21,230 --> 00:00:21,575
Wait,

11
00:00:21,575 --> 00:00:23,990
everyone's going? This is gonna be good.

12
00:00:24,140 --> 00:00:28,340
Sam's coming? Okay. I'm in.

13
00:00:28,490 --> 00:00:32,354
People often imagine that introverts hate parties.

14
00:00:32,504 --> 00:00:34,904
But that's not necessarily true.

15
00:00:35,054 --> 00:00:38,702
Alex likes people. He enjoys conversations. He just

16
00:00:38,702 --> 00:00:42,350
doesn't always want a room full of strangers

17
00:00:42,350 --> 00:00:45,542
and a hundred things happening at once.

18
00:00:45,692 --> 00:00:46,124
Hopefully,

19
00:00:46,124 --> 00:00:47,852
Sam gets there early.

20
00:00:48,002 --> 00:00:51,183
There's a difference between not enjoying people…

21
00:00:51,183 --> 00:00:54,819
and simply preferring a different kind of social

22
00:00:54,819 --> 00:00:55,274
experience.

23
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:56,314
Hey! What's up,

24
00:00:56,314 --> 00:00:58,212
man? How do you know the host?

25
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:02,960
Dude! I haven't seen you in weeks.

26
00:01:03,110 --> 00:01:04,454
Where have you been?

27
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:06,656
Trying to survive work.

28
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,864
Ryan naturally moves toward new conversations.

29
00:01:11,014 --> 00:01:14,206
Alex enjoys one conversation at a time.

30
00:01:14,356 --> 00:01:16,468
Neither is doing anything wrong.

31
00:01:16,618 --> 00:01:18,498
Psychologists describe introversion

32
00:01:18,498 --> 00:01:22,258
and extraversion as a personality dimension.

33
00:01:22,408 --> 00:01:25,096
Not two separate kinds of human beings.

34
00:01:25,246 --> 00:01:29,038
Most people fall somewhere along that spectrum.

35
00:01:29,188 --> 00:01:31,516
Bro! Come on. You're missing everything.

36
00:01:31,666 --> 00:01:33,418
I'm literally having a good time.

37
00:01:33,568 --> 00:01:35,152
Then why are you standing here?

38
00:01:35,302 --> 00:01:37,102
Because I'm talking to Sam?

39
00:01:37,252 --> 00:01:38,092
Fair enough.

40
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,256
Come meet these guys. They're hilarious.

41
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:43,104
Give me a second.

42
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:45,772
You always need a second.

43
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:48,608
And you never take one.

44
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:51,056
To Ryan,

45
00:01:51,056 --> 00:01:53,696
moving between people feels natural.

46
00:01:53,846 --> 00:01:54,816
To Alex,

47
00:01:54,816 --> 00:01:58,694
staying in one comfortable conversation feels perfectly enjoyable.

48
00:01:58,844 --> 00:01:59,948
But here's the problem.

49
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:04,468
People often judge someone else's experience using their

50
00:02:04,468 --> 00:02:07,136
own personality as the standard.

51
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,472
That was actually fun.

52
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:11,381
Okay…

53
00:02:11,381 --> 00:02:13,664
I think I need a minute.

54
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,288
There's a real experience people often describe.

55
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:20,260
They can enjoy the entire evening…

56
00:02:20,260 --> 00:02:23,020
and still feel completely exhausted afterward.

57
00:02:23,170 --> 00:02:26,897
One person described going to a party,

58
00:02:26,897 --> 00:02:29,026
talking, dancing, having fun…

59
00:02:29,176 --> 00:02:30,636
Then, hours later,

60
00:02:30,636 --> 00:02:32,582
suddenly hitting a wall

61
00:02:32,582 --> 00:02:34,528
and needing somewhere quiet.

62
00:02:34,678 --> 00:02:36,814
That doesn't mean the fun was fake.

63
00:02:36,964 --> 00:02:38,378
It means enjoyment

64
00:02:38,378 --> 00:02:42,148
and exhaustion can exist at the same time.

65
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:52,309
Alright,

66
00:02:52,309 --> 00:02:54,160
guys! I think we're wrapping up.

67
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:56,960
Already?

68
00:02:58,000 --> 00:02:59,728
I thought we'd hang out longer.

69
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,837
But the story doesn't end with Alex needing

70
00:03:02,837 --> 00:03:03,192
quiet.

71
00:03:03,342 --> 00:03:06,750
Ryan enjoys social interaction too.

72
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:09,406
And when the people around him leave,

73
00:03:09,406 --> 00:03:11,196
he may miss that connection.

74
00:03:11,346 --> 00:03:15,753
Research involving 949 people found that social support

75
00:03:15,753 --> 00:03:20,160
and lower social loneliness were associated with happiness

76
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:22,914
across different levels of extraversion.

77
00:03:23,064 --> 00:03:23,653
Interestingly,

78
00:03:23,653 --> 00:03:28,366
those relationships were even stronger among people with

79
00:03:28,366 --> 00:03:29,544
lower extraversion.

80
00:03:29,694 --> 00:03:32,526
Introverts need meaningful connection too.

81
00:03:32,676 --> 00:03:34,682
In another study,

82
00:03:34,682 --> 00:03:37,356
researchers followed 242 adults.

83
00:03:37,506 --> 00:03:40,898
Participants reported their everyday experiences five times a

84
00:03:40,898 --> 00:03:41,322
day,

85
00:03:41,322 --> 00:03:42,594
over two weeks.

86
00:03:42,744 --> 00:03:46,392
The researchers found that people higher in introversion

87
00:03:46,392 --> 00:03:48,672
reported fewer positive everyday experiences…

88
00:03:48,672 --> 00:03:51,408
and found those experiences less enjoyable.

89
00:03:51,558 --> 00:03:54,078
But there was an important detail.

90
00:03:54,228 --> 00:03:57,228
They didn't report more hassles overall.

91
00:03:57,380 --> 00:04:00,337
Being more introverted didn't simply mean having a

92
00:04:00,337 --> 00:04:01,076
worse life.

93
00:04:01,230 --> 00:04:04,734
It meant some everyday experiences were different.

94
00:04:04,884 --> 00:04:06,444
And remember…

95
00:04:06,444 --> 00:04:09,564
introversion isn't automatically shyness.

96
00:04:09,714 --> 00:04:11,934
Someone can be confident, funny,

97
00:04:11,934 --> 00:04:13,710
and comfortable around people…

98
00:04:13,710 --> 00:04:16,818
and still prefer a quieter social life.

99
00:04:16,968 --> 00:04:18,696
Hey. Where'd you disappear to?

100
00:04:18,846 --> 00:04:20,766
Just needed some quiet.

101
00:04:20,916 --> 00:04:22,356
I thought you weren't having fun.

102
00:04:22,506 --> 00:04:23,748
I was. Actually,

103
00:04:23,748 --> 00:04:25,818
I had a great time.

104
00:04:25,968 --> 00:04:27,048
Then why leave?

105
00:04:27,198 --> 00:04:29,066
Because having fun doesn't mean I want to

106
00:04:29,066 --> 00:04:29,766
do it forever.

107
00:04:29,916 --> 00:04:30,185
Honestly…

108
00:04:30,185 --> 00:04:32,340
I just didn't want the night to end.

109
00:04:32,490 --> 00:04:33,312
Next time,

110
00:04:33,312 --> 00:04:35,778
let's do something smaller. Just us.

111
00:04:35,928 --> 00:04:36,600
Deal.

112
00:04:36,750 --> 00:04:39,366
One person enjoyed the crowd.

113
00:04:39,516 --> 00:04:41,748
One enjoyed the quiet.

114
00:04:41,898 --> 00:04:44,730
One wanted the night to continue.

115
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:47,712
The other wanted a little time to recover.

116
00:04:47,862 --> 00:04:49,470
Neither was broken.

117
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:51,564
Neither needed fixing.

118
00:04:51,714 --> 00:04:54,954
So think about the last party you attended.

119
00:04:55,104 --> 00:04:57,944
Were you having the time of your life…

120
00:04:57,944 --> 00:05:00,783
or secretly waiting for the moment you could

121
00:05:00,783 --> 00:05:01,848
finally go home?

